114
00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:49.570
Mark Blackman: So, for those who know the consensus algorithm, the Hashgraph consensus algorithm.

115
00:21:49.750 --> 00:22:00.679
Mark Blackman: When an event is created, so events are messages that contain, transactions that need to come to consensus.

116
00:22:00.830 --> 00:22:07.059
Mark Blackman: And this is how events are how nodes share transactions with each other.

117
00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:11.259
Mark Blackman: And so, when a new event message is created.

118
00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:25.770
Mark Blackman: it is tied or anchored to multiple nodes. Today, it's the originating node creating the event, and then it finds one other

119
00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:38.449
Mark Blackman: one other node in the network to associate this event to. So, we call those nodes parents. So, the parents of the event is

120
00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:46.440
Mark Blackman: First, the originating node, we call that the self-parents, and then one other node, we ask, hey, can you

121
00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:51.100
Mark Blackman: Think of it almost like, co-sponsoring this event.

122
00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:57.079
Mark Blackman: So, that is called the other parent. And what this allows is really…

123
00:22:57.180 --> 00:23:04.249
Mark Blackman: This association is used for all the nodes to reconstruct the structure of the hash graph.

124
00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:21.329
Mark Blackman: But then it also helps seed the gossiping of these events, because the self-parents will start gossiping to other nodes, and the other parent will start gossiping this information to nodes.

125
00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:32.610
Mark Blackman: And so, think of this as kind of the roots of the event, and the… really the start of it being gossiped across the network.

126
00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:48.439
Mark Blackman: So what this hip is doing is saying, okay, we have one other parent, but what if we added multiple other parents? And what this ends up doing is, first, you have a self.

127
00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:56.650
Mark Blackman: and then the first other, and then multiple other parents. So, the event is when it's initially

128
00:23:57.080 --> 00:24:08.740
Mark Blackman: brought to… to the network, it now has multiple, think of it as sponsors, again, of this event. And so now, rather than just

129
00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:23.180
Mark Blackman: two parents starting to gossip about this event to the network. Now there's multiple, and this is a configurable… there is a new configuration item where you configure this,

130
00:24:23.320 --> 00:24:24.870
Mark Blackman: Somewhere, here we go.

131
00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:35.919
Mark Blackman: So you could configure… you could have 2, you could have three other parents. This is purely configurable. The default is the current, which is just one other parent.

132
00:24:36.100 --> 00:24:39.650
Mark Blackman: But because of this, When it's seated.

133
00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:57.680
Mark Blackman: It makes things, again, much faster with regards to when a transaction is first submitted to the network, to when it comes to consensus. And it, it allows it to come to consensus faster, which means there's less messages.

134
00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:05.039
Mark Blackman: Being gossiped, and then also it makes the… hash graph structure itself.

135
00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:17.029
Mark Blackman: Denser. So it… it reduces, it can reduce the, the memory of, of the long-term network, along with that.

136
00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:25.349
Mark Blackman: latency efficiency. So, this is a configurable item, it's backwards compatible.

137
00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:38.400
Mark Blackman: Again, this is just an extra efficiency layer that really doesn't interrupt any existing functionality, and it makes us faster and more efficient.

