202
00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:55.899
Richard Bair: what it is that you're trying to do in the hash graph. So, fundamentally in the hash graph, the fundamental problem that you're trying to solve for is branching. Branching is when one node is being dishonest relative to other nodes.

203
00:31:55.940 --> 00:32:04.389
Richard Bair: And this is an example here of some type of a hashcraft that's broken, in that Bob here is a liar and has created two different events.

204
00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:18.479
Richard Bair: And he shared some of those events with one person, and some with another person, and it… that's your… the fundamental problem that you're trying to solve for. And so there's this thing in the algorithms called Strongly Seeing, and the way that it works is that you have some event.

205
00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:27.700
Richard Bair: And that event needs to be able to see this other event Y, so X is trying to see Y, but it has to see it through

206
00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:34.279
Richard Bair: by… through a whole pile of other events that can also see why. So, X is saying, well.

207
00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:41.769
Richard Bair: Alice, you know, I'm Alice, and Alice has got some event in the set that can see why.

208
00:32:41.770 --> 00:32:46.779
Richard Bair: And Bob has an event that can see why, and Carol has an event that can see why, and…

209
00:32:46.780 --> 00:33:07.579
Richard Bair: Dave has an event that can see why, and even though Emily over here doesn't have an event that can see why, it's okay, because I have enough of them. And what this is proving is that these individual nodes absolutely have all seen why, because we have cryptographic hashes tying everything together, so we know that, you know, this, from the perspective of this event.

210
00:33:07.970 --> 00:33:14.710
Richard Bair: it can CY through over two-thirds of all the different members in our… in our network.

211
00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:29.399
Richard Bair: And that's, and this is the fundamental theorem here, is that if greater than two-thirds of the parties are honest, there exist solutions to the hash graph. So, in a more, like, if you were to look at the full graph.

212
00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:38.969
Richard Bair: you have some event, and can I see why? Well, through… through, here's one path that I can see it.

213
00:33:39.080 --> 00:33:57.899
Richard Bair: going through one of Carol's events, because this event has another parent here, this event has another parent here, this event has a self-parent here, this event had another parent here, and therefore X can see Y through these parent links. And if you go through

214
00:33:58.100 --> 00:34:14.080
Richard Bair: you have to do this for every single… basically, you're going to come… if you are doing the processing for this event, you're going to, for Alice, for Bob, for Carol, for Dave, and for Emily, you're going to try to see if there's a path that leads you back to why.

215
00:34:14.310 --> 00:34:27.379
Richard Bair: So this is the way that the algorithm works today. What multiple other parents does for us is, instead of having… like, if you look at this, we… because each event only has two parents, a self-parent and one other parent.

216
00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:30.349
Richard Bair: You have to build the graph up pretty high

217
00:34:30.380 --> 00:34:45.860
Richard Bair: before X can see Y. Because if this was… if this was our X guy, he obviously can see Y, but only directly, and not through anybody else. So then you have to have another event created, who now has a chance to see why this way, and this way.

218
00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:53.039
Richard Bair: But that's still only 2 of 5 members, so you need to have another event to even have a chance of seeing Bob with 3 of 5.

219
00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:59.729
Richard Bair: And you have to have another event to even have a chance of this event seeing it through 4 or 5. So, it's… it's like,

220
00:34:59.900 --> 00:35:06.230
Richard Bair: You have to create quite a few events, and you have to do quite a lot of processing in order to find why.

221
00:35:06.230 --> 00:35:23.760
Richard Bair: On the other hand, imagine if this event right here, or maybe this event right here, was able to have multiple other parents. It might have an arrow pointing here, and here, and here, and here, and they all are pointing to Y. So instead of having to go 4 levels up, I could just go 2 levels up, and I could still see Y.

222
00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:40.659
Richard Bair: And if I can do that, then it means that I can divide… this algorithm is used for dividing events into rounds, and rounds are the chunk of work that we do that then feeds into a block. Some whole number of rounds become a block. So, if we can do,

